    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d242">

                <pb facs="0748" n="711" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v16/pg711"/>

                <head>242. Memorandum From Secretary of State Vance to President Carter<anchor n="1" xml:id="d242-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1979-07-21">July 21, 1979</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>[Omitted here is material unrelated to Zimbabwe.]</p>
                <p>2. <hi rendition="#italic">
                        OAU Summit</hi>—The OAU Summit<anchor n="2" xml:id="d242-f2"/> ended
                    today with the passage of a resolution recognizing the Patriotic Front as the
                    “sole legitimate and authentic representative” of the people of Zimbabwe.<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d242-f3"/> The resolution also equates the lifting of
                    sanctions against Rhodesia with recognition of the Muzorewa government, and calls upon member states to apply
                    sanctions against any state which recognizes the Muzorewa government or lifts sanctions against it.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d242-f4"/> Apparently only the Ivory Coast and Zaire entered
                    reservations.</p>
                <p>Several African representatives at the meeting have commented that the resolution
                    should not be viewed as inconsistent with further negotiations between the
                    Patriotic Front and the Muzorewa
                    government. Many supported the resolution primarily as a means of halting <pb
                        facs="0749" n="712" xml:id="pg712"/> what they consider the “creeping
                    recognition” of the internal settlement by the UK and US.</p>
                <p>3. <hi rendition="#italic">Visas for Rhodesians</hi>—We are receiving an
                    increasing number of requests for visas from Rhodesians, both within and
                    supporters of the Muzorewa government. I
                    have instructed that we should consider each request on a case-by-case basis,
                    while generally giving more sympathetic consideration to those who carry
                    non-Rhodesian travel documents, such as UK passports.</p>
                <p>[Omitted here is material unrelated to Zimbabwe.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d242-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d242-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, National Security Affairs, Brzezinski Material, Subject
                    File, Box 29, Implementation of Presidential Instructions: 9–10/79. Secret.
                        Carter initialed the memorandum and
                    in the upper right-hand corner wrote: “Cy.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d242-f2" n="2" xml:id="d242-f2-content">
                <p>The OAU summit was held in Monrovia, Liberia,
                    July 17–21.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d242-f3" n="3" xml:id="d242-f3-content">
                <p>For a summary of the resolution, see <hi rendition="#italic">Keesing’s
                        Contemporary Archives, 1979</hi>, pp. 29841–29842.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d242-f4" n="4" xml:id="d242-f4-content">
                <p>
                    Carter wrote in the left-hand margin:
                    “I’ve noticed that Muzorewa has a poor
                    memory &amp; a vivid imagination re his U.S.
                    talks.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
